title: Character_Jushi
---
<<wait 0.5>>
<<play_sfx "doorbell">>
<<wait 1.5>>
<<show_character "jushi">>
<<wait 0.5>>
I: Hello, welcome in.
Jushi: Hello. I have an appointment with your shop owner, Shao Wei.
(The middle-aged woman with shoulder-length hair seems quite approachable, her head slightly bowed.
<<pause_dialogue>>
(I study the woman from the front, then tilt my head slightly left and right.)
<<pause_dialogue>>
(I can tilt my head left or right to see more of the customer from different angles.)
<<pause_dialogue>>
// Pause the communication interface here; resume the dialogue after Space.
<<pause_dialogue>>
I: You must be Lay Buddhist Hou. Everything is ready. Please have a look at the teapots first. We can go to the wild-vegetable restaurant later.
Jushi: How thoughtful is Shao. Though she has so much on her plate, she still made arrangements for my visit.
Jushi: One word from her and you're run off your feet. I really do feel quite apologetic.
I: Oh, not at all!
Jushi: I would like to pick out a teapot. [i]Dajue Temple[/i] is holding an event next month, and I want to gift it to a fellow volunteer.
I: Please sit and have some tea. I’ll bring over the teapots and the catalog.
(I lower my gaze and take out the catalog.)
<<pause_dialogue>>
(This catalog is the first assignment I have completed since Master Shao hired me.
Full of determination, I put my lenticular-card and graphic-design skills to work, collaging and photographing,
and sacrificed quite a bit of my time off to finish it.
She, meanwhile, spent a good deal of effort writing introductions for herself and the studio and commissioning a seal design.
Feeling the reassuring weight of the finished catalog in my hands,
I wonder privately why none of that weight has materialized as overtime pay.)
// Pause the communication interface here; resume the dialogue after Space.
<<pause_dialogue>>
(Hou accepts it in both hands, opens the catalog, and takes a sip of tea.
She turns a page gently and lifts one of the lenticular cards out to examine it.
While she studies it, my eyes sweep across the stock in the display cabinets.)
// Pause the communication interface here; resume the dialogue after Space.
<<pause_dialogue>>
Jushi: Shao Wei has an eye for these things, placing the [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i][/b] right at the front.
Jushi: After all, it is the founding ancestor of your craft.
(Hou presses her lips together and spends a little while turning the pages herself.)
// Pause the communication interface here; resume the dialogue after Space.
<<pause_dialogue>>
Jushi: Without him, where would this entire craft tradition be?
-> (Shao only put it first to make herself look respectful of history so as to sell more.)
    <<set $NotReal to true>>
    <<detour NotRealContent>>
    Jushi: After all that, why do you still place it on the very first page of your catalog?
    (Hou smiles gently.)
    <<jump Select1>>
-> I rather like this origin story.
    <<detour LikeStory>>
    <<jump Select1>>
===

title: NotRealContent
---
// Shared discussion of authenticity; returns to caller without an additional question.
<<set $GongDoubtKnown to true>>
I: The origins of the [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i][/b] are actually disputed.
I: In 1928, a local gentleman, Chu, picked up a teapot at a street stall in Suzhou. 
I: The two characters [i]“Gongchun”[/i] were inscribed on it.
I: To demonstrate his discernment and refined taste, he undertook a lengthy investigation of its provenance.
I: Besides him, Huang Binhong, a painter; Pan Zhiliang, a calligrapher;
I: and Pei Shimin, a teapot maker, all helped fan the flames of this teapot’s mythmaking.
I: The teapot eventually entered the collection of the [i]National Museum of China[/i],
I: but leading masters in the field, including [i]Gu Jingzhou[/i], 
I: questioned whether it had truly been made by Gongchun in the Ming dynasty.
I: Even the renowned craftsmen of the Wanli era supposedly could not match the skill this so-called founding master displayed in that one surviving piece.
I: Besides, before Shi Dabin, which potter even signed his own work in this way?
I: That sort of inscription looks more like the habits of literati projected onto artisans.
I: Perhaps those small landowners, intellectuals, and industrialists of Republican China, 
I: so eager to learn from the West, envied the humanist artists of the early European Renaissance.
(Hou presses her lips together. 
I touch my own mouth, realizing that I may have said too much.)
<<return>>
===

title: LikeStory
---
// Shared story and two choices, used in the opening and in GongNext.
<<set $LikeStory to true>>
I: A story that didn't go with the conventional narration of feudal ages would be unlikely to survive if it were entirely fabricated.
I: The people who made this history were not men of letters but monks and servants. 
I: The literati simply reaped the rewards.
I: I believe it matters little whether that young servant really was called [i]Gongchun[/i],
I: or Gongxia, Gongqiu, or Gongdong, for that matter. Nor does it matter which scholar he served.
I: What matters is the structure of the story.
I: Impoverished monks at Jinsha Temple made coarse earthenware for their everyday needs.
I: Mutual aid and the sharing of knowledge among ordinary people allowed those skills to reach household servants.
I: Through the intimate work of serving their masters, those servants brought their own creations before the eyes of scholar-officials.
I: Informal networks of circulation, years of development as everyday utensils, and complex relations of power all helped give birth to purple clay teapots.
I: Yet this long period of gestation rarely appears in the stories we tell.
[b]【I hear the call of [u]Courtesan[/u]】[/b]
Jushi: Quite so. How much of the work that nurtures something into being remains unseen?
Jushi: Just like bamboo. 
Jushi: We only notice it when it breaks through the earth in spring and shoots upward in the breeze,
Jushi: We give little thought to the years it spent quietly taking root underground.
Jushi: And when we hear a cicada sing, 
Jushi: we know nothing of its long years beneath the soil, or the effort of climbing into the trees.
-> And what about the women behind all those successful men competing in the workplace?
    <<detour OfficeMan>>
-> Cicadas must be the most rock-and-roll creatures on earth!
    <<detour RockNRoll>>
<<return>>
===

title: OfficeMan
---
I: Housewives managing homes and supporting everyone else are still portrayed as dependents living off another person’s earnings.
I: Has anyone calculated how much they save their families in domestic and care work? 
I: A live-in housekeeper alone can cost thousands of yuan a month.
I: And beyond the failure to value that labor, the exhaustion and physical harm of pregnancy and childcare
I: still go largely unseen, unrecognized, and unaddressed.
I: Yet the more quantifiable achievements of raising a child—education, employment, buying a home or a car—
I: are often credited to the father, as proof of his achievement and foresight.
I: And many women simply accept that account of things.
Jushi: My dear girl. 
(She presses her lips together.)
Jushi: Such is the selfishness of human desire.
Jushi: Even a man whose life has gone smoothly will complain that someone else has had it better.
Jushi: They never look downward to see how many have far fewer chances than they do.
Jushi: But tell me, how much of that complaint is truly lamenting injustice, 
Jushi: and how much is resentment at not having more privilege himself?
Jushi: We are fortunate to have been born in China. 
Jushi: Consider what some women endure in India. Have you seen [i]Pad Man[/i]?
Jushi: In some places women still face menstrual taboos and discrimination in marriage and widowhood. 
Jushi: It is terribly hard.
-> (Why does something about that make me uncomfortable?)
    I: But why should we use other people’s suffering as a measure of our own?
-> Mm...
I: Mm...
I: And what about the men in those societies?
I: Yes, what about the people responsible for these injustices?
I: In Buddhism, are they punished in the cycle of rebirth?
I: Is there some heavenly law, some scale, to judge kings, generals, slaveholders, and oppressors?
Jushi: Strictly speaking, punishment is not what the Buddha taught. 
Jushi: Only reaping what one sows belongs to the Buddha's teaching.
Jushi: [i]“Even after hundreds and thousands of kalpas, the karma we have made does not perish;[/i]
Jushi: [i]when causes and conditions come together, we ourselves receive its fruit.”[/i]
Jushi: When we look at so many events in society today, it is natural to think them deeply unfair.
Jushi: But when you consider the continuity of causes, conditions, and results, things are simply as they are.
Jushi: There is no unfairness. Only the unbroken flow of karmic fruition.
Jushi: People talk about cause and effect, but it is actually cause, condition, and effect. 
Jushi: The conditions in between are indispensable.
Jushi: We tend to look at things from only one side. 
Jushi: If we could approach them by the Middle Way,
Jushi: everything is simply the unfolding of causes, conditions, and fruits.
Jushi: Nothing is inherently good or bad. 
Jushi: Good and bad are judgments we impose on conditions.
Jushi: From the standpoint of dependent origination, things have no fixed qualities of their own.
Jushi: We call what pleases us good and what displeases us bad.
Jushi: Whether people, events, or objects, each is simply a phenomenon arising through dependent origination.
I: That is rather profound...
Jushi: You are a thoughtful young woman. (She presses her lips together.)
<<set $DeepTalk to true>>
<<set $LikeU to true>>
Jushi: I’ll send you a lecture by my master. Have a look, if you feel inclined.
// Game event placeholder: the Unity system adds the lecture video to WeChat and handles downloading/listening.
<<show_popup_wait "Day_1_HouJ">>
[b]【I hear myself calling [u]Ke Xiang[/u]】[/b]
<<return>>
===

title: RockNRoll
---
<<play_sfx "chanming" 5.5>>
I: They spend most of the year underground, then burst out in midsummer, singing at the tops of their lungs.
I: Silence, then roaring, clamoring, mating—and death.
I: Hahaha.
Jushi: What an unusual way of putting it. 
Jushi: Chatting with young women like you keeps us feeling young too. (She smiles warmly.)
I: I saw that online.
<<set $D1TalkedCicada to true>>
<<return>>
