title: Select1
---
Jushi: Well then, shall we choose a teapot?
I: Since it’s for a fellow volunteer, I think our [b]Lotus Reveries[/b] collection might be especially fitting.
Jushi: [b]Lotus Reveries[/b]?
I: It is in the catalogue. 
I: Master Shao has grouped all the teapots with Buddhist motifs under that collection.
(Hou glances at the table of contents, then starts leafing through the catalog.)
// Pause the communication interface here; resume the dialogue after Space.
<<pause_dialogue>>
I: Everything in the catalog is in stock. A custom clay blend or a special form would take rather longer.
Jushi: I'm on a somewhat tight schedule. 
Jushi: I see you have the [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i][/b] and the [b][i]Monk’s Cap Teapot[/i][/b] in stock.
I: I would recommend...
-> The [b][i]Monk’s Cap Teapot[/i][/b].
    <<jump SengMao>>
-> The [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i][/b].
    <<jump GongChun>>
===


title: SengMao
---
<<set $TriedSengMao to true>>
(Hou turns to page four of the booklet.
<<pause_dialogue>>
She reads the description I revised word by word under Master Shao’s kindly but unmistakable pressure.
She handles the lenticular card I made as if it were a small curiosity, tilting it up and down.
// Pause the communication interface here; resume the dialogue after Space.
<<pause_dialogue>>
I hurry to retrieve the teapot from the display cabinet and set it before her.
She holds its handle in one hand and supports the base with the other, examining it from different angles in the light.
I tilt my head slightly and watch her.)

I: I have heard that Buddhism values [i]seeing things as they truly are[/i].
I: There is a great deal of controversy surrounding the [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i][/b].
I: Some even suspect that its story borrows the prestige of temples and the history of monks making pottery to lend itself legitimacy.
<<if not $GongDoubtKnown>>
    Jushi: What do you mean?
    <<detour NotRealContent>>
<<endif>>
I: That is why I would recommend this [b][i]Monk’s Cap Teapot[/i][/b] for your friend.
I: First, since she volunteers at [i]Dajue Temple[/i], she must already know quite a bit about the purple clay culture.
Jushi: My dear girl. (Hou smiles gently.)
Jushi: [i]Dajue Temple[/i] was designed under [i]Master Hsing Yun’s guidance[/i]. 
Jushi: Its public facilities and sheltered walkways are remarkably thoughtful.
Jushi: That spirit of consideration draws volunteers from all over.
Jushi: My friend is not even from Yixing.
I: Oh! I see.

-> Then perhaps I could recommend the [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i][/b] instead. <<if not $TriedGongChun>>
    <<jump GongChun>>
-> Mm... <<if $TriedGongChun>>
    <<jump FailEnd>>
-> I think this one is quite suitable.
    I: It is assembled from six [i]joined clay panels[/i].
    I: It is extremely difficult to make. 
    I: Only the elder sophisticated masters we work with still have that skill, so there is very little stock in all of Yixing.
    I: It is rare and valuable. 
    I: Some artisans make such pieces simply to display their technique, not even to sell them.
    I: Our master considers it an advanced collector’s piece, recommended only to serious enthusiasts and connoisseurs.
    I: Still, among customers buying these higher-end pieces,
    I: many are reluctant to give or display objects with religious motifs. So they do not sell quite as readily.
    I: If you give one to your friend, she will know at a glance that you spent a great deal. 
    I: She will know how much you value her.
    Jushi: I'm afraid I don't quite agree with that.
    (Hou smiles gently.)
    Jushi: That I gladly host her and give her my time and care—that is how I show she matters to me.
    Jushi: What does money have to do with it?

    <<if $LikeU and not $TriedGongChun>>
        -> Perhaps I could show you the [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i][/b] again.
            I: You’re right. That was poorly put. 
            I: It was what they taught us in training, and I repeated it without thinking.
            (Hou smiles gently and leafs through the catalog again.)
            I: Ah, my mouth gets ahead of me. It is not even what I believe myself.
            <<set $WrongFirst to true>>
            I: I would still recommend the [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i][/b].
            <<jump GongChun>>

        -> Mm...
            <<jump FailEnd>>
    <<else>>
        -> Mm...
            [b]【I hear the call of [u]Hermes[/u]】[/b]
            <<jump FailEnd>>
    <<endif>>
===


