title: GongNext
---
<<if not $GongDoubtKnown>>
    <<detour NotRealContent>>
<<endif>>
I: Even if that surviving piece was not truly made by Gongchun in the Ming dynasty, 
I: it does not follow that today’s [b][i]Gongchun-style Teapot[/i][/b] is without value.
I: At the very least—
I: at the very least, it offers us a glimpse into the early circulation, economic foundations, and cultural setting of purple clay teapots.
I: We remember people of the past in the ways available to us today, carrying their ideas into practice.
I: Using present-day techniques to imperfectly retrace what earlier hands once did
I: —it feels almost like a conversation across time.
I: You have never met the Buddha in this life. Does that make his wisdom any less able to guide you?
I: The volunteer work you do, the prayer beads you wear—have these not changed across generations?
I: Even if forms are reinvented and old artifacts disappear, does that mean the meaning of an object vanishes with them?
I: I would rather think you are speaking with the people who once used these objects and the artisans through whose hands they passed.
<<set $GongMeaningKnown to true>>
Jushi: (She presses her lips together.)
<<if not $LikeStory>>
    Jushi: [i]"Not grasping at outward appearances."[/i] Then what do you make of the Gongchun story?
    <<detour LikeStory>>
<<endif>>
<<if $LikeU>>
    <<jump HatTalk>>
<<else>>
    <<jump SucEnd>>
<<endif>>
===

title: HatTalk
---
Jushi: To tell you the truth, I have never been especially fond of the [b][i]Monk’s Cap Teapot[/i][/b].
Jushi: My father did collect one years ago, though I couldn't say where it is now.
I: You strike me as someone who believes things should be put to use. 
I: A piece collected only for display may not appeal to you.
(Hou smiles gently.)
Jushi: It isn't merely a matter of [i]"few desires and contentment"[/i]. 
Jushi: Look at this lid. What does it remind you of?
I: It looks like... Please forgive my ignorance, but it reminds me of the hat worn by the monk Tang Sanzang in the television series [i]Journey to the West[/i].
Jushi: Ignorance? You observed very well. That shape, 
Jushi: with five lotus petals in front and a small cap behind, is called [i]Vairocana Crown[/i].
(Hou presses her lips together.)
Jushi: Of course, over the centuries, contact among different peoples and Buddhist traditions has blurred some of these distinctions.
Jushi: But broadly speaking, that headdress was reserved for senior Buddhist masters in the Han Chinese tradition.
Jushi: Ordinary monks wore the [i]Guanyin Hood[/i].
Jushi: The lotus-shaped one with five Buddha images—one on each section—is the [i]Five-Buddha Crown[/i], used in Tibetan Buddhism.
I: I see.
<<jump FinalConve>>
===

title: FinalConve
---
// The player may ask any combination of three questions. Hide previously chosen options in the UI.
<<if $AskedMiZong and $AskedDengji and $AskedOurs>>
    <<jump SucEnd>>
<<endif>>
-> Is it that you dislike [i]Vajrayana[/i]? <<if not $AskedMiZong>>
    <<jump MiZong>>
-> Is it the hierarchy you object to? <<if not $AskedDengji>>
    <<jump Dengji>>
-> One, two, three, four, five, six... Oh! But the shape of our teapot... <<if not $AskedOurs>>
    <<jump Ours>>
-> Mm... Shall we wrap it up, then?
    <<jump SucEnd>>
===

title: MiZong
---
<<set $AskedMiZong to true>>
Jushi: What I object to is hierarchy.
Jushi: And esoteric Tantric Buddhism most of all.
Jushi: These days, well over ninety percent of people simply absorb institutional religious dogma.
Jushi: Especially in Tantric sects. 
Jushi: If you dare doubt the guru's word, you're told you'll be condemned for eternity.
Jushi: Even stepping on the guru’s shadow is treated as a grave transgression.
Jushi: Senior monks lording it over junior monks. This is the reality of religious institutions.
Jushi: Such preferential treatment. It operates no differently from any worldly social clique.
Jushi: Decadent, really.
<<jump FinalConve>>
===

title: Dengji
---
<<set $AskedDengji to true>>
Jushi: Institutional religion is not the same thing as the Dharma taught by the Buddha. 
Jushi: True Dharma is truth itself, and it can be verified for oneself.
Jushi: It is not blind devotional worship, nor is it begging for blessings.
Jushi: What matters is cultivating the mind. 
Jushi: Using everyday events to refine it, turning inward to examine yourself whenever life tests you.
Jushi: Only your own effort can bring a wish to fruition. 
Jushi: Put your faith in outside forces and you become easy to deceive.
Jushi: Many religious organizations today use their position to swindle people out of money.
Jushi: I've changed course now.
Jushi: Stay too long inside institutional religion, and it dulls one's clarity.
Jushi: That kind of faith is really blind credulity, not genuine right faith.
Jushi: I have come to understand that the true Dharma can be tested. 
Jushi: It is universal truth, valid in any era and any place.
Jushi: If you ask me to judge institutional religion today, I would call it a tool of rulers.
Jushi: [i]Drinking poison to quench a thirst.[/i]
Jushi: Buddhism did not spring from nowhere, nor did it take its present shape overnight.
Jushi: There were the [i]śramaṇa movements[/i], exchanges and disputes with [i]Brahmanical traditions[/i], 
Jushi: One must learn to distinguish the Dharma itself from what other religions and the history of transmission have added to it.
Jushi: Of course, religion can offer temporary comfort to people in great pain.
Jushi: An anesthetic, perhaps. But it cannot in itself uproot suffering or carry one beyond birth and death.
I: And how is your own practice coming along?
Jushi: That is hardly something one can measure. But I am still working at it.
<<jump FinalConve>>
===

title: Ours
---
<<set $AskedOurs to true>>
Jushi: Yes, this piece of yours is modeled after the hexagonal tall Monk's Cap
Jushi: After [i]Shi Dabin's[/i] design, is it?
Jushi: But in the proper design, one of the six sides serves as the spout.
Jushi: That is to say, only five of the six panels around the lid are lotus petals. One side is left open.
Jushi: This teapot, on the other hand, has hired the Five Dhyani Buddhas a sixth colleague. 
Jushi: How on earth is Shao Wei curating her stock?
(Hou smiles gently.)
I: Oh dear... 
I: That is something we ought to pay more attention to speaking of preserving tradition. 
I: Thank you for pointing it out.
<<jump FinalConve>>
===

title: SucEnd
---
// Pre-sale safeguard: both Gongchun discussions must have occurred before checkout.
<<if not $GongDoubtKnown or not $GongMeaningKnown>>
    I: Wait, I would still like to explain the provenance of this [b][i]Gongchun-style Teapot[/i][/b] properly.
    <<jump GongChun>>
<<endif>>
Jushi: It is getting late, my dear. I ought to be going.
Jushi: I shall take your advice and choose this [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i][/b].
Jushi: Thank you for looking after me. I know I've put you to quite a bit of extra work.
[b]【I hear the call of [u]MBTI[/u]】[/b]
Jushi: You are so young... (She pats your shoulder gently.) Younger even than my son.
Jushi: He is studying abroad. He is going to Columbia for his master’s degree.
[b]【I hear the call of [u]Baochai[/u]】[/b]
-> Mm...
    I: Mm...
-> I was an international student once myself. I studied the humanities and art.
    Jushi: Ah, that explains why you are such a lively conversationalist. A fine young person.
    Jushi: My son doesn't much care for pondering these sorts of things, though he is very kindhearted and gentle.
    Jushi: I enjoy speaking with young people, sharing what little I have learned in my few extra years.
    Jushi: He has no interest in the Dharma, even though he could pick some up in his religion electives in college. 
    Jushi: Still, he always listens patiently until I've finished.
    (Hou presses her lips together.)
Jushi: Is everything ready? Let us wrap it up. Nothing too elaborate. No need to waste materials.
I: Of course.
(I take out the [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i][/b] and prepare to ring up Hou’s purchase.)
<<jump JushiD1_ScanGongChun>>
===

